  "page1": {
    "title": "When Sour Meets Bitter, Something Calms Down",
    "subtitle": "The quiet science behind antacids and bee stings.",
    "body": "Bite into a fresh lemon and your whole mouth puckers from the sharp, sour taste. Touch a bar of soap and it feels strangely slippery and a little bitter. The lemon is a clear example of an <u>acid</u>, while the soap is a base. These two families of chemicals are like natural opposites, and each one is fairly strong on its own. But something interesting happens when you mix an acid and a base in just the right amounts.\n\nWhen an acid meets a base, the two <u>cancel each other out</u>. Scientists call this a <mark>neutralization</mark> reaction. The sharp acid and the harsh base react together and form ordinary water and a mild salt. The angry, stinging quality of both of them simply fades away. We can measure this change with a number called pH. A strong acid sits low on the pH scale, a strong base sits high near the top, and a neutral mix lands calmly in the middle. Mixing the two pushes that number gently back toward the safe center.\n\nThis quiet balancing act shows up all around you every day. When your stomach makes too much acid, an antacid tablet is a base that neutralizes it and stops the pain. When a bee sting leaves a bit of acid under your skin, a dab of baking soda, a gentle base, can ease the burn. Farmers even add powdered lime to sour soil so that healthy plants can grow. Pool keepers carefully test the water and add chemicals to keep its pH safe for swimmers. The big lesson is not that acids or bases are somehow bad. It is that two strong opposites, brought together with care, can create a gentle <blank> that neither one could ever reach alone.",
    "illustration": "../../assets/illustrations/2026-07/05.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Acid plus base makes water and salt — the pH returns to the calm middle.",
    "gloss_extra": [
      { "term": "base", "ko": "염기" },
      { "term": "pH", "ko": "pH (산성도 척도)" },
      { "term": "salt", "ko": "염 (화학)" }
    ]
  },
